# Summary of changes for run 093746e6-770f-408c-9ed5-2304b2e4a24c
I wrote and published all five deliverables. The article and paper never mention proof assistants, file paths or formal identifier names. `demo.py` ran successfully. I could not compile the LaTeX file because `pdflatex` isn't available, and I couldn't render the four plotting scripts because matplotlib isn't available (they were only checked for syntax).

- **`ARTICLE.md`** (about 2,000 words) tells the story through a detective searching a row of lockers. It covers:
  - the step posterior and the committed cost (block mass times mean slot);
  - why trusting a calibrated oracle is optimal (the layer-cake argument), and the anti-calibrated counterexample (cost \(23/10\) reversed vs. \(47/10\) committed);
  - the crossing law \(\Phi \le 1/(3s)\) and its area form \(\mathrm{AUC} \ge 1 - 1/(3s)\);
  - how the single-window law \(w \le \alpha - (1 - 2/(3s))\) comes back as a special case;
  - the chord rule for adding windows, monotonicity, the outer-window bound, and why the "named windows only" version fails (\(1/40\) vs. \(7/10\)).

- **`RESEARCH_PAPER.md`** (about 3,400 words) is the formal paper: abstract, contributions, model and definitions, and full statements with proof sketches of every result. It also includes an oracle-design linear-programming example, the two counterexamples, the fully worked \(M = 6\) instance (\(\Phi = 5/18\), finite speedup \(7/6\), continuum speedup \(6/5\)), algorithms, discussion and open problems.

- **`RESEARCH_PAPER.tex`** is a shorter amsart version (2,000 words or so) with theorem environments. It contains every theorem, lemma, definition and both counterexamples, but some remarks and expository passages are cut down.

- **`demo.py`** uses exact fractions and the standard library only. It checks the claims in the article and paper:
  - all 720 orders of the worked example (minimum \(13/6\), reached by 12 orders);
  - the identity \(\Phi + \mathrm{AUC} = 1\), the single-window law, the chord identity and the naive-gate failure;
  - convergence of the finite speedup to \(6/5\);
  - the outer-window bound on 2,000 random menus.

  These checks are numerical evidence, not proofs.

- **`PACKAGE.json`** has every schema field filled in:
  - domain "Computation", since "Combinatorics" isn't an allowed value;
  - one demo, three algorithms with pseudocode and code (all three ran), and four plotting scripts;
  - one interactive widget, the "Reliability Curve Explorer": you drag windows on a unit square and watch the area and verdict change live;
  - a step-by-step `interactive_layout` with collapsible proofs and external links, and the Phase A future directions.

